const spikerData = {
  color:"#a68a5c", bg:"#0f0d09",
  title:"The Unravelling of Intelligibility: Five Bifurcations",
  subtitle:"Hasan Spiker's account of the five severances that split Christendom apart, piece by piece, into what now presents itself as neutral, universal, 'just modern' civilisation",
  intro:"Walks through five historical \"splits\" in Western thought since the medieval period — of law from spirit, reason from revelation, nature from grace, and so on — arguing that each split, though it looked like progress, actually broke something whole into two pieces that no longer fit back together.",
  sections:[
    { id:"overview", label:"The Book and Its Central Claim", body:`Hasan Spiker is a Cambridge-trained philosopher who works across two traditions at once: on the western side, close study of Plotinus, Dionysius the Areopagite, Kant, and Hegel; on the Islamic side, sustained immersion in the school of Ibn 'Arabi and later kalam theology. His earlier books — Things as They Are: Nafs al-Amr and the Metaphysical Foundations of Objective Truth (2021), The Metacritique of Kant and the Possibility of Metaphysics (2022), and Hierarchy and Freedom (2023) — all circle the same underlying concern: whether modern western philosophy's account of truth, reason, and human autonomy actually holds together on its own terms, or only appears to by quietly borrowing coherence it can no longer justify. The Unravelling of Intelligibility, the first volume of a planned multi-book series, applies that same scrutiny to the whole of modern civilisation at once.

The book's opening move is to name a genuine irony at the heart of the modern world: the "neutral," "universal," "scientific" civilisation that emerged from a very specific corner of Western Europe has been exported so thoroughly and so successfully that it no longer needs to call itself Western at all — it can simply call itself modern, full stop, the shared horizon everyone is presumed to be moving toward regardless of where they started. And the people most likely to insist on this today, Spiker observes, are very often the direct descendants of the nations that were colonised by the civilisation in question — a pattern this app's own colonial epistemology module documents from a different angle. Rather than treating modernity as a simple, unified achievement of reason over superstition, Spiker's central explanatory device treats it as the accumulated residue of five specific historical fractures — he calls them bifurcations or severances — each of which took something that earlier Christian and pre-Christian civilisation held together as one integrated whole, and split it into two separated, independently operating halves. Modernity, on this account, is not what is left after superstition was removed. It is what is left after five specific joints were cut, one by one, over roughly two thousand years, and the resulting pieces were later reassembled into a system that no longer remembers it was ever whole.` },

    { id:"hierarchy-freedom", label:"Companion Work: Hierarchy and Freedom", body:`Spiker's shorter 2023 study Hierarchy and Freedom: An Examination of Some Classical Metaphysical and Post-Enlightenment Accounts of Human Autonomy makes a natural companion to the bifurcation framework above, because it supplies the rigorous philosophical proof behind exactly what Bifurcation 5 — morality severed from ontology — describes historically. Where The Unravelling of Intelligibility traces how the severance happened across two thousand years, this earlier, denser 120-page monograph asks a narrower and sharper question: once morality and freedom are cut loose from any objective hierarchy of being, does the resulting idea of "autonomy" actually work, even on its own terms?

Spiker opens by naming a specific contemporary reflex worth stating plainly: the word "hierarchy" triggers near-automatic negative associations in modern secular culture — serfdom, caste, feudal power interests, oppression dressed up as order — while "freedom" and "autonomy" are treated as unambiguously noble achievements that modernity uniquely discovered and uniquely protects. His book's real target is this asymmetry itself. He argues, first, that Enlightenment and empiricist accounts of freedom — he takes Locke's Essay Concerning Human Understanding as the clearest case — try to ground human autonomy in rational self-determination, but do so from within a materialist, immanentist ontology that has no objective, ranked hierarchy of goods for reason to be rationally oriented toward. Once that hierarchy is removed, "rational self-determination" has no way left to distinguish a genuinely rational choice from an arbitrary impulse dressed up in the language of reason — reason itself becomes just one more psychological state alongside desire, with no standard above it to which either could be held accountable. Spiker's claim is not merely that this account of freedom is thin or unsatisfying. He argues it formally fails on its own terms: it cannot deliver the very thing — freedom as rational self-determination, rather than freedom as bare arbitrary will — that it sets out to establish.

Against this, Spiker sets the classical Platonic and Neoplatonic account of hierarchy, exemplified above all in Plotinus's Enneads VI.8 ("On Free Will and the Will of the One"). Here, reality is understood as a graded hierarchy of participation, each level receiving its reality and its proper end from what stands above it, culminating in the Good or the One. On this account, freedom is not the absence of hierarchy or the absence of an ordering constraint — it is what hierarchy itself makes possible in the first place: the soul becomes more rationally self-determining, not less, the more fully it aligns with and participates in its proper place within that ordered whole. Autonomy, in other words, is not achieved by escaping hierarchy; it is achieved by rightly locating oneself within it, oriented toward the same summit that grounds the hierarchy as a whole.

The book's second aim runs alongside the first: to rehabilitate the very concept of hierarchy against what Spiker treats as a pervasive and lazy polemical caricature — that hierarchy is nothing but an historical euphemism for power interests, imposed social ranking, and domination dressed up in metaphysical language. He argues this picture mistakes accidental historical abuses of hierarchical social arrangements for the logical and metaphysical structure hierarchy actually names: a strict, ordered relationship of dependence and participation, not a power ranking at all, and one that — properly recovered — is precisely what a rootless, purely voluntarist idea of freedom is missing.

This connects directly back to Bifurcation 5 above. Kant's autonomous, self-legislating will is, on Spiker's reading across both books, the exact historical and philosophical descendant of the immanentist ontology Hierarchy and Freedom subjects to close logical scrutiny — and the shorter book's conclusion sharpens the historical claim into a stronger, purely philosophical one: it is not simply that morality got detached from ontology at a particular point in European intellectual history, but that once detached, the very autonomy Enlightenment ethics prizes above all else stops being available to ground on its own immanentist terms at all. One honest caveat is worth naming plainly: this is a dense, technical, and explicitly polemical work of comparative metaphysics rather than a work of history, and its central conclusion — that Platonic hierarchy grounds freedom more successfully than Lockean empiricism — is a serious philosophical claim that deserves serious engagement from defenders of liberal autonomy, not simply acceptance on the book's own say-so.` },

    { id:"b1-law-spirit", label:"Bifurcation 1 — Law and Spirit (Pauline Christianity)", body:`The first severance, on Spiker's account, opens within Christianity itself, in the theology of Paul. Paul, a Greek-speaking Jew from a Pharisaic background, reoriented the young Christian movement around salvation through faith in Christ rather than through continued adherence to Mosaic law — a shift that made real sense as a missionary strategy, since it opened the new movement to Gentile converts who had no prior attachment to Jewish legal practice. Whatever its pastoral motivation, the theological effect was to prise apart something that had previously functioned as one thing: external legal obligation and internal spiritual life, sharia-like practice and spiritual disposition, no longer treated as two faces of a single reality but as separable, with the inward, spiritual side increasingly awarded priority over the outward, legal side.

Spiker traces the long afterlife of this first severance across the following two millennia: once law and spirit are conceptually prised apart at the root of Western Christianity, the door is open, centuries later, for law to go on being law even after it has been fully detached from any spiritual or theological grounding at all — which is exactly what happens in the eventual secularisation of Western legal systems, no longer justified by reference to divine will but by reference to positive law, procedure, and human legislative authority alone. The contrast Spiker draws to Islamic civilisation is direct: shariah (the sacred law) and tariqah (the spiritual path) are conceived as mutually reinforcing rather than separable, the law understood as the practical, embodied expression of the same spiritual reality the path cultivates inwardly, neither one awarded priority as the "real" religion with the other demoted to secondary status.` },

    { id:"b2-spiritual-temporal", label:"Bifurcation 2 — Spiritual Power and Temporal Power (Early Middle Ages)", body:`The second severance widens within medieval Western Christendom itself: the split between spiritual power, vested in the Church, and temporal power, vested in kings and emperors. The clearest institutional flashpoint was the Investiture Controversy of the eleventh century, in which the Papacy and European monarchs fought at length over who actually held the authority to appoint bishops — a struggle that was never really about clerical appointments alone, but about which of the two powers, spiritual or temporal, ultimately governed the other. The eventual settlement institutionalised something that earlier Christendom had not needed to formally separate: two distinct spheres of authority, sacred and secular, each with its own domain, its own logic, and its own claim to jurisdiction.

Once this division is locked into Western institutional life, Spiker argues, the long-run trajectory becomes almost inevitable: the temporal sphere gradually accumulates independent legitimacy of its own, no longer requiring the spiritual sphere's sanction to govern, until the modern secular state emerges as the temporal power's final, total victory — a state that relegates religion to private life precisely because the two spheres were institutionally prised apart from one another eight or nine centuries earlier. Islamic civilisation, again, offers Spiker's contrasting model: the classical Caliphate embodied a unified vision in which temporal authority was itself rooted in and legitimated by spiritual standing, not a separate, competing power sharing the same territory — the difference between Din (a total way of life encompassing governance) and "religion" in the modern privatised sense already documented at length in this app's own religion module.` },

    { id:"b3-faith-reason", label:"Bifurcation 3 — Faith and Reason (Later Middle Ages)", body:`The third severance concerns the relationship between faith and reason. High Scholasticism, epitomised by Thomas Aquinas, represents the last serious Western Christian attempt to hold faith and reason together as complementary sources of the same truth — Aristotelian philosophy pressed into the service of Christian theology, reason understood as faith's ally rather than its rival. Spiker treats this synthesis as fragile from the outset, and the Enlightenment as the moment it finally comes apart for good: rationalism sets itself up as sufficient on its own terms, revelation and faith increasingly dismissed as incompatible with genuine intellectual inquiry rather than integrated with it, until a fully secular epistemology emerges in which reason alone is treated as adequate for understanding reality, with no need for, and often active suspicion toward, any residual role for revealed truth.

Spiker's Islamic contrast here runs through figures like al-Ghazali and Ibn Taymiyyah, both of whom, in different ways, maintained a working balance between 'aql (intellect/reason) and naql (transmitted revelation) rather than allowing either one to fully eclipse the other — a balance Spiker suggests allowed classical Islamic civilisation to produce serious advances across multiple disciplines without needing to sever its intellectual life from its theological roots to do so, in direct contrast to the Western trajectory from Scholastic synthesis to Enlightenment rupture.` },

    { id:"b4-nature-subject", label:"Bifurcation 4 — The Natural World and the Knowing Subject (Early Modernity)", body:`The fourth severance is the one most readers will recognise most readily: the Cartesian split between the natural world, as an external, objective reality, and the knowing subject, as the separate, internal locus of perception and thought. Descartes's dualism is the hinge: nature is reconceived as a mechanism, a collection of extended objects governed by mechanical laws and available to be measured, manipulated, and mastered, with no inherent meaning, life, or sacred quality of its own; the thinking subject stands entirely apart from this mechanised nature, the one thing in the whole picture that still possesses interiority, and the two — extended nature and thinking subject — are no longer understood as two aspects of one integrated created order, but as two fundamentally different kinds of thing facing off against each other.

Spiker treats this severance as the direct enabling condition for the eventual dominance of empirical science as the sole legitimate route to real knowledge, and for a broadly materialist picture of nature that has no room left for nature's sacred or symbolic dimension — the very phenomenon this app's own Scientism module traces through Comte and the Vienna Circle. Against this, Spiker sets the Quranic picture of the natural world as ayat — signs pointing beyond themselves to their Creator — with the Quran repeatedly inviting reflection on nature as simultaneously an intellectual and a spiritual exercise, holding together exactly what the Cartesian severance pulled apart: the world studied and the world as meaningful sign.` },

    { id:"b5-morality-ontology", label:"Bifurcation 5 — Morality and Ontology (Early Modernity)", body:`The fifth and final severance separates morality from ontology — ethics from the basic question of what is ultimately real. Its clearest expression is Kantian ethics, which grounds moral obligation in human autonomy and the rational will's own self-legislated law, rather than in any external metaphysical or divine reality; an action's rightness, on this account, is settled by the structure of practical reason itself, not by its correspondence to some deeper fact about being. Once morality is grounded in the autonomous rational will alone, severed from ontology, the door opens to the moral relativism that follows across the nineteenth and twentieth centuries: if ethics answers only to the self-legislating will rather than to reality itself, then differing wills can quite consistently legislate differing moralities, with no remaining fact of the matter to adjudicate between them.

Spiker's Islamic contrast here grounds morality directly in the nature of being: ethical conduct is judged not merely by its outcomes but by its alignment with haqq — ultimate reality and truth themselves — so that morality and ontology are never allowed to come apart in the first place. An action's rightness, on this view, is not legislated into existence by an autonomous will; it is discovered, in the same way a fact about reality is discovered, because moral value is treated as a genuine feature of how things actually are rather than a structure the will imposes upon an otherwise morally neutral universe.` },

    { id:"synthesis", label:"Putting the Five Together — Why 'Neutral' and 'Universal' Are the Wrong Words", body:`Read together, the five bifurcations tell a single continuous story rather than five unrelated anecdotes. Law separates from spirit within Christianity itself; spiritual authority separates from temporal authority within Christendom's own institutions; faith separates from reason within Scholasticism's collapse; nature separates from the knowing subject in Descartes; morality separates from ontology in Kant. Each severance takes something that functioned as one integrated whole and leaves behind two independently operating halves — and modernity, as Spiker presents it, is simply what remains once all five splits have occurred and the resulting fragments have been recombined into a system that has almost entirely forgotten it is built from fragments at all.

This is precisely why "neutral" and "universal" are, on Spiker's account, the wrong words for the resulting civilisation, and precisely why this book sits so naturally alongside this tab's own central argument. What calls itself simply "modern" — post-religious law, the secular state, reason emancipated from revelation, mechanised nature, autonomous will-based ethics — is not the absence of a specific inheritance. It is the fully specific, five-times-fractured inheritance of one particular civilisation's internal history, wearing the language of neutrality only because the fractures that produced it have been forgotten by the people currently living inside the result — precisely the mechanism this tab's own primer describes as a plausibility structure sustaining a worldview long after anyone remembers why it once made sense to hold. Spiker's volume one demonstrates the framework's explanatory power by tracing how all five severances converge to fracture something as concrete and everyday as the built urban environment, craft, and ordinary interpersonal relationships — with further volumes promised on progress and the self-creating individual, immanentism and the intelligibility of nature, faith and reason and 'science' specifically, gender and family, power politics, and postcolonialism, applying the same five-bifurcation framework across each domain in turn.` },
  ],
  genealogy:`The five severances in historical sequence: Pauline theology separates law from spirit (1st century) → the Investiture Controversy and the medieval Church-State settlement separate spiritual from temporal power (11th–12th centuries) → Scholastic synthesis (Aquinas, 13th century) attempts and ultimately fails to hold faith and reason together, a failure completed by Enlightenment rationalism (17th–18th centuries) → Cartesian dualism separates the natural world from the knowing subject (17th century) → Kantian ethics separates morality from ontology (late 18th century) → the accumulated residue of all five severances is exported globally through colonialism and re-presented as simply "modern," "neutral," and "universal" rather than as one civilisation's specific and fractured inheritance.`,
  intersections:[
    { with:"Religion / Din as Operating System", text:"Spiker's first two bifurcations — law split from spirit, and spiritual power split from temporal power — are the detailed historical mechanics behind this app's religion module's broader claim that 'religion' as a bounded, privatised category is a specific Western post-Reformation invention rather than a universal fact about all religious civilisations." },
    { with:"Colonial Epistemology", text:"Spiker's observation that the descendants of colonised peoples are often modernity's most devoted defenders is the same phenomenon this app's colonial epistemology module documents as the deepest and most durable form of colonial control — control that no longer needs external enforcement because it has been internalised as simply 'how things are.'" },
    { with:"Scientism", text:"Spiker's fourth bifurcation — nature split from the knowing subject in Descartes — is the specific historical hinge this app's scientism module picks up several centuries later, tracing how a mechanised, disenchanted nature becomes available for the exclusively empirical, science-alone account of knowledge that Comte and the Vienna Circle would later formalise." },
    { with:"Meta-Worldview / Plausibility Structure", text:"The book's implicit argument — that a civilisation built from five specific historical fractures can nonetheless present itself as the neutral, unmarked default — is a rigorous, historically detailed demonstration of this tab's own central claim, translated into the specific vocabulary of severed law, power, reason, nature, and morality rather than the vocabulary of worldview and paradigm used elsewhere on this tab." },
  ],
  analogies:[
    { title:"The Shattered Vase Reassembled as a Mosaic", text:"A vase is dropped and shatters into five large pieces. Rather than being glued back into a vase, the pieces are rearranged flat and cemented into a mosaic, which is then presented to newcomers as an original, intentional artwork — nobody mentions it used to be something else entirely, something that held water, something whole. Modernity, on Spiker's account, is the mosaic: five specific historical fractures (law/spirit, spiritual/temporal power, faith/reason, nature/subject, morality/ontology) rearranged into a system that presents itself as simply how civilisation looks, with no memory that each seam in the pattern used to be a single unbroken piece." },
    { title:"The Five Load-Bearing Walls Removed One Renovation at a Time", text:"A house undergoes five separate renovations across many decades, each removing a different interior wall to open up the floor plan, each renovation justified on its own local terms and each seemingly harmless at the time. No single renovation collapses the house. But a visitor who only ever sees the final open-plan result, never having seen the original walled structure, will experience the open floor plan as simply what a house is, with no sense that five specific structural decisions — each individually documented, each individually reversible in principle — produced it. Spiker's five bifurcations are these five renovations; modern secular civilisation is the open floor plan mistaken for the house's original, natural design." },
    { title:"The River Diverted Five Times Upstream", text:"A river's final course through a valley can look, from where it currently flows, like the obviously natural path water would always have taken through that terrain — until an engineer's survey reveals five separate diversions upstream, each dug at a different point in history for a different specific reason, each one redirecting water away from a channel it previously shared with something else. The final course feels inevitable only to someone who never saw the five diversions being dug. Spiker's project is that engineer's survey applied to the whole of modern civilisation: showing that its 'natural,' 'neutral' course was cut, five separate times, by specific historical hands." },
  ],
};

